    description: "Patan Patola Heritage Museum, run by the award-winning Salvi family, opened in 2014 across 3,000 sq ft and three floors documenting the real, centuries-old art of Patan Patola double-ikat weaving. Tradition holds that in the 12th century, Solanki king Kumarapala invited 700 patola-weaving families from Jalna in Maharashtra to settle in Patan; today, the Salvi family alone has genuinely kept the craft alive across 35 generations, specializing in double-ikat since the 11th century — a technique that dyes both the warp and weft threads before weaving so the pattern locks in place on both sides. A single genuine Patan Patola saree can take five months to a year to complete, and the Salvis remain, distinctively, the only family still using entirely natural dyes — indigo, turmeric, madder root, pomegranate skin, marigold — in the process.",
