    description: "Girnar Hill, rising to 3,672 feet just outside Junagadh, is one of India's great pilgrimage climbs: a genuine stone staircase of 9,999 steps leading past 866 Hindu and Jain shrines built between the 8th and 15th centuries. About two-thirds of the way up sits the Jain temple cluster, its mosaic domes and stupas centred on the 12th-century Neminath Temple, dedicated to the 22nd Tirthankara — Jain pilgrims know Girnar as Mount Neminath. Higher still, at the 9,999th step, is Dattatreya Paduka, the summit shrine to Guru Dattatreya, revered as a combined incarnation of Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva. Most pilgrims start the climb before dawn, and a ropeway (among Asia's longest) now offers a way up to the Amba Mata temple partway for those not attempting the full stone staircase on foot.",
