    description: "Chand Baori, in the village of Abhaneri, was built around the 8th-9th century CE by King Chanda of the Nikumbha dynasty as a genuine, practical answer to water scarcity in this arid part of Rajasthan. Roughly 3,500 narrow steps arranged across 13 storeys descend 20 metres in a precise, symmetrical \"upside-down pyramid\" — widely cited as India's largest and deepest stepwell — creating a striking geometric play of light and shadow that has made it a favourite location for photographers and filmmakers (it appears in The Dark Knight Rises and The Fall). A multi-storey pavilion adjoining the steps carries genuine carvings of Hindu deities including Ganesha and Durga, and the site historically served a dual purpose: a dependable water source through the dry season, and a naturally cool gathering place for both locals and royalty. The adjacent Harshat Mata Temple rounds out a visit to this often under-visited but genuinely extraordinary site.",
