    description: "Kuldhara was founded around 1291 CE by Paliwal Brahmins who migrated from the town of Pali, genuinely turning a patch of inhospitable desert into a thriving settlement through real engineering skill — stepwells, underground water channels, and rainwater-harvesting systems that let them farm successfully in arid conditions. Positioned along historic caravan trade routes near Jaisalmer, the Paliwals, a strictly vegetarian community that valued education, prospered here for over 500 years. Then, in 1825, Kuldhara and roughly 84 neighbouring Paliwal villages were reportedly abandoned overnight — local tradition holds this followed threats from Salim Singh, a powerful Jaisalmer state official, against a village chief's daughter, though the precise historical cause remains debated among scholars. Whatever the full truth, the sudden, total abandonment of dozens of villages is real and well-documented, and today Kuldhara's roofless stone houses and lanes stand preserved as a protected monument, giving a genuinely rare, intact glimpse of a complete medieval desert settlement.",
