    id: "kuttanad",
    name: "Kuttanad",
    state: "Kerala",
    hidden: true,
    tagline: "The Only Place in India Where Rice Is Farmed Below Sea Level",
    description: "Kuttanad, spread across parts of Alappuzha, Pathanamthitta, and Kottayam districts, is genuinely one of the very few places on Earth where farming happens below sea level — paddy fields sit 2.5 to 3 metres beneath it, held dry by mud-wall dikes (bunds) that farmers have built and maintained for over 150 years. The region is a floodplain where four rivers (Pampa, Achencovil, Manimala, and Meenachil) converge into Vembanad Lake, and its name is traditionally traced to \"Chuttanad\" (burnt spot), after a fire once swept through forest here during the Chera dynasty era. The traditional farming system, called Puncha Krishi, precisely times planting and harvesting around seasonal tides using a real network of canals and pumps, and in 2013 the UN's Food and Agriculture Organisation formally recognised Kuttanad's below-sea-level farming as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System — a genuine, still-living example of centuries-old hydraulic engineering sustaining an entire regional economy and landscape.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Kerala_Backwaters_Kuttanad_Paddy_fields.JPG/1280px-Kerala_Backwaters_Kuttanad_Paddy_fields.JPG",
    heroImage: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Kerala_Backwaters_Kuttanad_Paddy_fields.JPG/1280px-Kerala_Backwaters_Kuttanad_Paddy_fields.JPG",
