    description: "Kottayam's name is traditionally traced to the Malayalam kotta (fort) and akam (inside) — \"Kottaykkakam,\" or \"inside the fort\" — reflecting a fortified past that has long since given way to a real, distinctive identity as Kerala's \"Land of Letters,\" a title earned through the city's outsized role in Malayalam-language education and printing since the early 19th century. Geographically, Kottayam genuinely straddles two worlds: Vembanad Lake and its backwaters, paddy fields, canals, and wetlands to the west, and streams flowing down from the Western Ghats feeding directly into that same lake system — a real hydrological link between highland and coastal Kerala in one district. As with the rest of the backwaters, Portuguese, Dutch, and later British colonial powers reshaped Kottayam's waterways with canal expansions and embankments for trade, infrastructure whose traces are still genuinely part of the landscape today alongside the district's modern role as a backwater-tourism hub in its own right, distinct from nearby Alleppey and Kumarakom.",
