    description: "Abbey Falls, about 8 km from Madikeri, drops some 70 feet through genuine coffee estates, pepper vines, and thick forest — a real, working plantation setting rather than a standalone nature spot. Its name traces back to the British colonial era, when it was first called Jessy Falls by Coorg's first chaplain, in honour of his daughter, before being renamed Abbey Falls after Indian independence, when Coorg was a favoured summer retreat for British planters and officials. After independence, the land passed to the Government of India before being sold to Neravanda B. Nanaiah, who genuinely transformed it from natural forest into cultivated coffee and spice plantations — making Abbey Falls one of the few Indian waterfalls set within an actual, still-operating coffee estate rather than a protected forest reserve.",
