    description: "Talacauvery, on Brahmagiri hill near Bhagamandala in Kodagu district, sits at 1,276 metres and is genuinely revered as the birthplace of the Kaveri River — one of India's seven sacred rivers. A small rectangular tank, the Cauvery or Brahma Kundike, marks the exact spring believed to be the river's true source; the water is said to disappear underground shortly after emerging, resurfacing some distance away near Bhagamandala where it joins the Kannike and Sujyoti rivers at the sacred Triveni Sangama confluence. Hindu tradition holds that Brahma himself performed a yagna here to seek forgiveness for his sins, and that the small pond created during that ritual became the river's origin — a real, still-active pilgrimage belief rather than a purely historical curiosity. Beyond the religious significance, the site's hilltop setting in the Brahmagiri hills offers genuinely sweeping views over the Western Ghats, especially from the nearby summit trek.",
