    tagline: "Two 120-Metre Limestone Monoliths, Carved by Nature Over Centuries",
    description: "Yana, deep in the Western Ghats of Uttara Kannada district, centres on two massive black limestone (karst) rock formations — Bhairaveshwara Shikhara, standing 120 metres tall, and the smaller Mohini Shikhara at 90 metres — genuinely sculpted over centuries by natural weathering rather than by human hands, despite their almost deliberately monumental appearance. Local tradition connects the two peaks to the mythological story of the demon Bhasmasura's encounter with Shiva and Mohini (an avatar of Vishnu), with the rocks themselves said to represent the two figures. A cave temple at the base of Bhairaveshwara Shikhara holds a self-manifested Shiva lingam, drawing thousands of devotees each year for a ten-day Maha Shivaratri celebration that culminates in the Maha Mastaka Abhisheka. Genuinely remote and reached only via a real forest walk from the nearest road, Yana rewards visitors with a landscape that looks almost otherworldly rather than a conventional pilgrimage-site visit.",
