    description: "Mirjan Fort, on the banks of the Aghanashini River about 22 km from Gokarna, was built in the 16th century by Rani Chennabhairadevi of the Tuluva-Saluva dynasty, who ruled the Gersoppa region under the Vijayanagara Empire from 1552 to 1606 and controlled territory spanning North and South Kannada into parts of South Goa. The Portuguese, who traded extensively with her, genuinely called her \"Raina da Pimenta\" — the Pepper Queen — for her real, dominant control of the Arabian Sea pepper trade, alongside cinnamon, nutmeg, ginger, and sandalwood exports to Europe through the port at Mirjan. Spanning 4.1 hectares, the fort is genuinely one of India's strongest, with one main gate and three smaller ones, interconnected wells, and a defensive moat; excavations here have turned up real Portuguese gold coins from the era of Viceroy Conde de Sarzedas, cannonballs, Chinese ceramics, and Islamic-script clay tablets. Later in life, Chennabhairadevi adopted Jainism and commissioned several Jain temples in the region — a genuine second legacy alongside her military and mercantile one.",
