    description: "Anegundi, just across the Tungabhadra River from Hampi in Koppal district, genuinely predates the Vijayanagara Empire and served as its first, 14th-century capital before the seat of power shifted to Hampi itself. Its history stretches back much further still — the settlement was drawn into the Ashokan Empire's boundaries by the 3rd century BCE, and over more than 3,000 years passed through the Satavahanas, Kadambas, Chalukyas, Rashtrakutas, Delhi Sultanate, Vijayanagara, and Bahamani rule. Hindu tradition holds Anegundi to be the mythical kingdom of Kishkindha from the Ramayana, ruled by the monkey king Sugriva, with nearby Anjaneya Hill traditionally believed to be Hanuman's own birthplace. The Gagan Mahal, once the royal quarters of the Vijayanagara rulers before Hampi's rise, and the atmospheric, part-ruined Huchappayana Matha Temple by the river — with its lathe-turned black stone pillars and carved dancer panels — are among Anegundi's real surviving structures, together making it a genuinely quieter, less-visited counterpart to Hampi's more famous ruins.",
