    description: "Brindavan Gardens, in Mandya district about 12 km from Mysuru, was conceived by Sir Mirza Ismail, then Diwan of Mysore, after the completion of the nearby Krishnarajasagara (KRS) Dam across the Kaveri in 1924 — a dam built under Maharaja Krishnaraja Wodeyar IV and guided by the legendary engineer Sir M. Visvesvaraya. Construction of the gardens began in 1927 and took five years, involving the Department of Horticulture, before opening to the public at the end of 1932. Spread across 60 acres, the terraced layout was genuinely modelled on Kashmir's famous Shalimar Gardens, reflecting Mirza Ismail's own real, well-documented passion for horticulture. Its musical fountain show, choreographed to music, has become the gardens' signature evening draw, pulling in visitors alongside its terraced lawns, flowerbeds, and views over the dam itself — a real fusion of engineering achievement and deliberate landscape artistry from 1930s Mysore.",
