    description: "Shivanasamudra Falls, on the Kaveri River in Mandya district, is really two segmented waterfalls — Gaganachukki (90m) and Barachukki (69m) — split around an island where the river divides. Its real historical significance goes well beyond scenery: nearby, at Shivanasamudra, Asia's first hydroelectric power station was switched on 30 June 1902 by Mysore's British Resident, Donald Robertson, with an initial output of just 700 kilowatts, first proposed in 1898 by French engineer Alain Chartier Joly de Lotbinière and championed by Mysore's Dewan, Sir K. Seshadri Iyer. That modest plant sent power along a genuinely record-setting 147 km transmission line to the Kolar Gold Fields — then the longest such line anywhere in the world — and by 1905 extended supply to Bangalore, making it the first Indian city with electric power. The plant remains active today, a real, still-functioning piece of 19th-century engineering history sitting quietly beside one of Karnataka's most dramatic natural waterfalls.",
