    hidden: true,
    tagline: "Gokarna's Remotest Beaches, Reachable Only on Foot or by Boat",
    description: "Half Moon Beach and Paradise Beach are the two southernmost, most remote stops on Gokarna's real five-beach trek — a coastal walking route running from Paradise Beach north through Half Moon, Om, Kudle, and finally Gokarna's main beach. Half Moon, named for its crescent shoreline, is genuinely one of the quieter and less-commercialised beaches in the area, framed by forested hills and reachable only by the coastal trek or by boat. Paradise Beach, further south still, is similarly boat-or-trek-only, and its shoreline genuinely cannot comfortably hold more than about 25 people at a time. Paradise Beach's history carries a real, sometimes-glossed-over chapter: it became a well-known winter base for international backpackers on the old hippie trail, and for a period gained a reputation as a spot where cannabis was openly sold — a trade that authorities eventually cracked down on, dismantling the informal shacks associated with it. Today both beaches are visited for their genuine remoteness and natural beauty rather than that history, best reached as part of the wider beach-trekking route.",
