    description: "Tungabhadra Dam, near Hospet about 20 km from Hampi, has real roots stretching back to the 1860s, when British engineers first began investigating a dam across the Tungabhadra River as a response to famine conditions in the region. Negotiations and planning dragged on for roughly eighty years before construction finally began in 1949, as a genuine joint project between the erstwhile Hyderabad State and Madras Presidency, with engineer Dr. Thirumala Iyengar as its principal architect. Completed and inaugurated in 1953, it became, after India's post-independence state reorganisation, a shared project between Karnataka and Andhra Pradesh — a real, still-active cross-border infrastructure arrangement. As a genuine multipurpose dam, it serves irrigation, hydroelectric generation, and flood control simultaneously, and its landscaped gardens and viewing points, especially dramatic when the gates are opened during monsoon, make it a popular add-on to a Hampi trip beyond its practical engineering role.",
