    description: "Silent Valley National Park, spanning about 237.52 sq km of the Nilgiri sub-cluster of the Western Ghats in Palakkad district, protects one of the last genuinely undisturbed tracts of tropical evergreen rainforest anywhere in peninsular India, drained by the Kuntipuzha river and forming part of the wider Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve. Its real, defining story is the Save Silent Valley movement — a twelve-year campaign, from 1973 to 1985, that stopped a Kerala State Electricity Board hydroelectric dam from submerging roughly 8.3 sq km of this rainforest. Led by poets, teachers, students, and local villagers — with poet-activist Sugathakumari's \"Marathinu Stuthi\" (Ode to a Tree) becoming a rallying symbol, and figures like ornithologist Salim Ali and scientist M.S. Swaminathan lending their voices — it became independent India's first major grassroots environmental protest. Prime Minister Indira Gandhi intervened in 1980 to suspend the project pending environmental review, the dam was formally abandoned in 1983, and Silent Valley was declared a National Park in November 1984, inaugurated the following September by Prime Minister Rajiv Gandhi — a genuine landmark in Indian conservation history that today's forest, still standing, directly commemorates.",
