    description: "Eravikulam, right next to Munnar town, was historically a hunting reserve maintained by the European planters who developed Munnar's tea estates from the late 19th century, its game including Nilgiri Tahr, sambar, gaur, tiger, and leopard. Real, sustained pressure from planters, naturalists, and scientists led the government to declare it the Eravikulam-Rajamalai Wildlife Sanctuary in 1975, and it became a national park in 1978 — genuinely Kerala's first. Its purpose today centres on the endangered Nilgiri Tahr, a mountain goat found almost nowhere else on Earth: the total wild population across the entire Western Ghats is estimated at only around 3,000 animals, and Eravikulam alone holds the largest single concentration, somewhere between 700 and 800 — a real conservation success story that makes sightings on the park's short, well-maintained trail near-guaranteed. The park also serves as the gateway to Anamudi, South India's highest peak, adding a real mountaineering dimension to what is primarily a wildlife-viewing destination.",
