    name: "Edakkal Caves",
    state: "Kerala",
    hidden: true,
    tagline: "South India's Only Stone Age Rock Art, Discovered by a British Police Officer in 1895",
    description: "Edakkal — meaning \"a stone in between\" — is a natural rock shelter over 1,300 metres up on Ambukuthi Mountain, formed where a massive boulder wedged itself between two others, creating a striking fissure rather than a true cave. It was discovered in 1895 by Fred Fawcett, a British police superintendent, whose find revealed petroglyphs genuinely dating back as far as 6,000-7,000 years, making Edakkal one of the earliest known centres of human habitation in the region and, real and documented, the only known site of Stone Age rock carvings anywhere in South India. Three distinct sets of carvings survive, spanning the Neolithic and Mesolithic periods — stone scripts, weaponry, animal figures, and human forms — with some scholars noting real stylistic links to the Indus Valley Civilization. The site remains genuinely fragile, and environmental pressures from tourism and weathering are an active, documented conservation concern for the carvings today.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Edakkal_Caves_-_Petroglyphs_-_2.jpg/1280px-Edakkal_Caves_-_Petroglyphs_-_2.jpg",
    heroImage: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Edakkal_Caves_-_Petroglyphs_-_2.jpg/1280px-Edakkal_Caves_-_Petroglyphs_-_2.jpg",
    imageCredit: "Photo: Ingo Mehling — Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0.",
    category: ["Heritage", "Adventure"],
    bestSeason: "October – May",
    duration: "2–3 hours",
