    name: "Banasura Sagar Dam",
    state: "Kerala",
    hidden: true,
    tagline: "India's Largest Earthen Dam, Named for a Demon King's Penance",
    description: "Banasura Sagar Dam, built in 1979 across the Karamanathodu River (a Kabini tributary) near Kalpetta, is genuinely India's largest earthen dam and Asia's second-largest, standing 38.5 metres tall and stretching 776 metres long, built using compacted soil and rock fill rather than concrete. Part of the wider Banasura Sagar Project, it supports the Kakkayam hydroelectric project alongside real irrigation and drinking-water supply for the surrounding region. Its name traces to the Asura king Banasura, son of Mahabali, who tradition holds performed severe penance in these surrounding hills — a genuine mythological layer beneath the modern engineering. The reservoir's real visual signature is the cluster of small islands that emerge when it's full, a scenic effect that has made Banasura Sagar a popular Wayanad stop for boating and short island treks alongside its practical role as regional infrastructure.",
