    description: "Surajkund — literally 'Lake of the Sun' — is an artificial kund built against the backdrop of the Aravalli hills, its amphitheatre-shaped embankment constructed in a semicircular form. It's said to have been built by the Tomar king Suraj Pal in the 10th century; Suraj Pal, a sun worshipper, also built a Sun temple on its western bank, whose ruins remain visible today. Just 8 km from South Delhi, Surajkund is a favourite picnic spot, its complex including a landscaped garden and a second pool, Siddha Kund. But it's most famous for hosting the internationally acclaimed Surajkund International Crafts Mela — held every year from 1st to 15th February — a fair celebrating Indian handicrafts, handlooms, and folk traditions, drawing artisans and visitors from across the country and abroad.",
